{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to the ENISA AI Cybersecurity Challenges report, how does GDPR Article 5's principle of security relate to the AI threat landscape, and what role does Article 32 play?", "answer": "GDPR Article 5 establishes security as a principle for processing personal data, making it a necessity, while Article 32 requires security measures to scale with risk, both influencing the AI threat landscape.", "evidence": [{"page": 9, "quote": "The General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) establishes, under Article 5, security as a principle when processing personal data. This is an advanced role for security and an important conceptual shift from the past, when security was a mere technical-organizational provision on top of the processing operation."}, {"page": 10, "quote": "Article 32 of the GDPR calls for security measures that must scale-up according to the risk of varying likelihood and severity for the rights and freedoms of data subjects."}]},

  {"question": "What are two strategic choices regarding security in AI systems discussed in the ENISA AI Cybersecurity Challenges report?", "answer": "The report suggests embedding security by design for trust in AI and promoting security certification to encourage a security culture among economic actors.", "evidence": [{"page": 11, "quote": "two strategic choices could be explored concerning security:\n\n· reflecting on the necessary functional role that security may have in the faultless functioning of an AI system, and on how to embed security since the very early stage of design (security by design) in order to create trust in new AI applications;\n\n· considering the positive role that security certification may have in promoting a culture of security among economic actors, especially considering that certification may relieve the stakeholders from the complexity of the managerial decision between being idle and accepting security risks, or investing for reinforcing security."}]},

  {"question": "In the ENISA AI Cybersecurity Challenges report, what is the main focus of the ENISA AI Threat Landscape (AI TL) and which AI approach is emphasized?", "answer": "The main focus is on cybersecurity of AI, with particular emphasis on Machine Learning technologies.", "evidence": [{"page": 11, "quote": "In the context of the ENISA AI Threat Landscape (AI TL), the main focus of the work will be on cybersecurity of AI since secure AI is the foundation for any further work on AI. Only when AI itself is secure can we use it in a trustworthy manner and can we further utilise it for additional cybersecurity operations. Ethics of AI remain outside the scope of this work, since this was one of the focus areas of the EC High-Level Expert Group on AI (AI HLEG) 19 ."}]},

  {"question": "According to the ENISA AI Cybersecurity Challenges report, what is the purpose of the AI lifecycle reference model described in Chapter 2?", "answer": "The reference model establishes a conceptual framework for understanding AI system assets and their relationships, aiding in systematic threat analysis.", "evidence": [{"page": 14, "quote": "The purpose of having a reference model is to establish a conceptual framework ensuring shared understanding of the assets composing an AI system and their significant relationships. This facilitates the assignment of owners to different assets on one hand and on the other hand provides a systematic, structured way of analysing relevant security threats."}]},

  {"question": "Which AI lifecycle phase, as defined in the ENISA AI Cybersecurity Challenges report, involves identifying the business context and metrics for an AI application?", "answer": "The Business Goal Definition phase involves understanding the business context and metrics for the AI application.", "evidence": [{"page": 17, "quote": "Prior to carrying out any AI application/system development, it is important that the user organization fully understand the business context of the AI application/system and the data required to achieve the AI application's business goals, as well as the business metrics to be used to assess the degree to which these goals have been achieved."}]},

  {"question": "In the ENISA AI Cybersecurity Challenges report, which technique is used to increase the diversity of training data for image classification tasks?", "answer": "Data augmentation, such as applying translations, rotations, and blurring filters, is used to increase diversity in image classification training data.", "evidence": [{"page": 18, "quote": "One task where data augmentation is used by default is image classification, where data can be augmented by for instance applying translations, rotations and blurring filters."}]}
]}
